  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1235 AM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nSaturday-Sunday...\n\nForecast remains on track for this weekend. The upper high will \npersist overhead, leading to weakened flow aloft and a general lack \nof any large-scale forcing. Recycled monsoon moisture beneath the \nhigh will allow for continued afternoon showers and thunderstorms, \nthough activity will be confined to the higher terrain. Heavy \nrainfall and flash flooding will continue to be our primary concern, \nmainly over burn scar areas. Meanwhile, the eastern plains will \nremain dry and hot, with high temps increasing above 100 degrees.\n\nNext Week...\n\nNot much change to the long-range forecast at this time. The broad \narea of high pressure will remain over Colorado and the surrounding \nstates, allowing for more hot temperatures and daily rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms, thanks to recycling monsoon moisture. \nFlash flooding will continue to be a primary concern for the burn \nscars, while the eastern plains will continue to see triple-digit \nhighs for a few more days. Precip coverage will be an upcoming \nforecast challenge, as long-range guidance has a few weak \ndisturbances passing around the high throughout the week. These \ncould send some weak fronts down across the eastern plains, \nspreading precip chances over the rest of our CWA for a few \nafternoons. Will have to wait and see whether the timing of these \ndisturbances remains consistent in later model runs. With the \nincreased chance for widespread precipitation, temperatures may \nactually decrease a few degrees, bringing us firmly back into the \ndouble digits (but it'll still be hot).",
